From 4d50fd08889ac7841d27f49fb42cb42686863c19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Durham <amdnative@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 14:30:34 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] hyundai: gas interceptor: speed-scheduled pedal gain + hold
 table, jerk-based rise limit (drive 12e/12f launch surge)

PEDAL_SCALE 0.36 was the highway gain; the measured gain at 1-15 m/s is 2-3x higher, so launches rode the
launch ceiling and surged to 2.1-2.5 m/s^2 for a 1.15-1.3 request at 8-12 m/s. Gain now [0.10..0.36] at
[0..22] m/s with a matching hold table in command units; identical to the old law at >= 22 m/s. The fixed
0.75/s rise slew becomes 2.0 m/s^3 * gain(v). Cap 0.35, launch ceiling and panda ceiling unchanged.

Hold table standstill anchor 0.04 -> 0. This car has gas-only authority (no brake actuator), so any throttle
at a standstill against a zero/negative request is uncommanded motion; the old 0.04 commanded ~0.5 m/s^2
(0.04 * the ~13/unit low-speed gain). A zero/negative request at 0 m/s now commands exactly 0 pedal (the
ENABLE=0 all-zero frame). The 0-5 m/s hold ramps linearly 0 -> 0.08, so >= 5 m/s and the highway law are
unchanged, and the pull-away path is untouched (0.10 gain at 0 m/s: 0.5 -> 0.05, 1.0 -> 0.10). Pinned by
test_standstill_hold_is_zero and test_standstill_zero_request_sends_zero_frame.

Review fix (should-fix): test_launch_no_surge_on_fitted_plant comments why the ceiling check is needed --
at ~3 m/s a >= 1.2 m/s^2 request sits exactly on the launch ceiling (0.168) under both the old and the new
law, so the fitted plant's request+~0.2 headroom is ceiling-driven, not gain-driven; the surge bound only
applies where the ceiling is not the active limiter.
